---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/browse-abandonment';
const title = "Follow product interest without inventing purchase intent";
const desc = "A product view is a weaker signal than a cart or checkout. Design browse recovery around identity, permitted tracking and useful product help before deciding whether any message should be sent.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/use-cases" class="link text-sm font-bold">← All use cases</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-700">{desc}</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">
      <h2>Establish eligible identity</h2>
      <p>Document how the browsing event is tied to a known contact, what tracking and messaging permissions apply and how those states reach the sender. Do not send to a guessed identity or merge devices solely because they share an address or network. Keep anonymous browsing outside this workflow until a supported identity relationship exists.</p>
      <h2>Choose a meaningful entry rule</h2>
      <p>Use your own buying cycle to propose an entry condition and evaluate it against accidental visits, staff traffic and repeated refreshes. Two views or a fixed dwell time are not universal purchase-intent thresholds. Record the product and event time, and set an expiry so old interest does not generate surprising messages.</p>
      <h2>Give stronger signals priority</h2>
      <p>Exit browse recovery when the shopper adds a cart, starts checkout, purchases or opts out. Define which system owns that transition and what happens if events arrive out of order. Do not let a delayed product-view event restart a browse message after an order.</p>
      <h2>Offer help matched to uncertainty</h2>
      <p>Choose a short product explanation, sizing help or compatibility information that the catalog can support. Avoid claiming the shopper abandoned a cart they never made. If the item becomes unavailable, suppress or use an explicitly designed restock request path rather than linking to an unusable purchase page.</p>
      <h2>Proposed supplier exercise</h2>
      <p>Replay a known shopper viewing a product, then buying on another device before the queued message. Also replay an anonymous visitor and a withdrawn permission. Inspect identity and exit logs. Evaluate complaints and additional contribution alongside conversions before expanding the audience or introducing incentives.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
